#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "2acf7f4e-1d5d-56e5-8478-9611a3afde73" :question/math-academy-id "e-12766" :question/problem "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\n \n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n![](images/15/159dcc0eb98804f6d4d281603de45cf00922023ce1d427ff73a7188bea38e40d.svg)\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2,$ and $5$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{ 1,2,5 \\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cc45cc10-61e3-5cb7-babc-7253a5158c4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12767" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n\nIII. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ad5497-4c7b-5cee-9dd2-73a3d6490141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{a,b,c\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422943} {:answer/id #uuid "3de264c5-1f35-5d8e-a444-50f18125c80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{a,b,d\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422944} {:answer/id #uuid "542ca6b3-fe35-53d2-9cfb-a7e7acc49f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{c,e\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422945} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e7470-cbcd-5fc5-978a-946545586f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{d,e\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422952} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cb7c81-b3d9-57ae-a707-bdeda79e3767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{a,b,c,d\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de264c5-1f35-5d8e-a444-50f18125c80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{a,b,d\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422944} :answer-field/id #uuid "d41cebf3-62a2-5784-a1f4-13fc481b665c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3c4858f-5a41-55b3-bb89-5356156d99e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-172706" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" :question/worked-solution "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $a,b,$ and $d$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{a,b,d\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092339 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Indicator Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d76cebc-b6fb-5524-8283-b87a8706d0a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089796} {:answer/id #uuid "a035904c-4fe1-5a9f-a9bf-e49750aa30b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089797} {:answer/id #uuid "f97d5917-25f1-5529-9b0f-beee8799d9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089798} {:answer/id #uuid "48009762-6e64-51c1-8c34-e8d675fca580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089799} {:answer/id #uuid "678c699c-69d3-50d3-949b-5005fc07d7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a035904c-4fe1-5a9f-a9bf-e49750aa30b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089797} :answer-field/id #uuid "88da4f35-85b0-53f0-a65f-db9edf474b77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bab9e63e-a883-5b00-876f-108808a6cc8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-172723" :question/problem "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n\nIII. $5\\in A\\cup B$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2438f833-da6b-51c4-8043-ebd69eed10a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{p,r\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501530} {:answer/id #uuid "778d7d49-8554-5fbf-a462-170fd01ecb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{r,q,t\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501689} {:answer/id #uuid "867d0a72-1046-54b9-b414-cd5dbfa660a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{p,r,s\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501724} {:answer/id #uuid "a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{q,t\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501767} {:answer/id #uuid "b21165e0-d461-5c86-99ff-1d2317a6798b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{r,s\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06e7d00-9ae9-55f2-aa6a-f5a68db01aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{q,t\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501767} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb8e7c2-0363-5cb5-8d8a-c3481b00ae75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.dif